jb_customer: Hey @support — after rotating our API key our web app users are getting 401 invalid_api_key. Server-side calls using the new key succeed, but the browser calls fail. Rotation happened ~10m ago. Any pointers?

maria_support: Hi! Sorry about that — a couple quick checks: are you sending the key in Authorization: Bearer ... or x-api-key? Can you paste a request example or a curl repro? Also did you rotate via Console or the API? :thumbsup:

jb_customer: We use Authorization: Bearer. Rotated via console. Backend (node) with same token works fine. In browser network tab the request shows Authorization: "Bearer ****OLD***" (we mask in prod) — so looks like the old key is being sent from the client. We have a service worker that proxies requests.

maria_support: That points at a client-side cache or service worker retaining the old token. Quick repro: DevTools -> Application -> Service Workers -> Check the console for any "Serving from cache" logs. Also try Disable cache (Network tab) + Hard reload and test.

jb_customer: Confirmed: service worker console shows "Serving cached request for /v1/chat" and the header dump shows Authorization: "Bearer <OLD_TRUNC>".

devin_engineer: Yep — common pattern. Service worker or an in-browser cache can hold an old Authorization. Repro steps for us to verify from your side: 1) curl from your machine (bypasses browser):

curl -v -H "Authorization: Bearer NEW_KEY_REPLACED" https://api.redwood.inference/v1/chat -d '{"input":"hello"}'

Expect 200. If curl returns 401, paste the -v output (response headers). If curl is OK, it's definitely client-side.

maria_support: Also check for invisible characters when you stored the key in client storage (leading/trailing whitespace). Try copying the key into an editor and inspect length/quotes. Browser storage sometimes saves a newline.

jb_customer: Curl returns 200. So it's the SW. We forgot to update SW code that injects Authorization into outgoing requests. The SW still injects the previous key from indexedDB.

rohit_infra: Example pattern that causes this (make sure you remove old header or update fetch clone):

self.addEventListener('fetch', event => {
  const req = event.request;
  // BAD: reusing old headers without clearing
  let newHeaders = new Headers(req.headers);
  newHeaders.set('Authorization', 'Bearer ' + (localStorage.getItem('apiKey') || ''));
  const newReq = new Request(req, { headers: newHeaders });
  event.respondWith(fetch(newReq));
});

If your SW reads the key from indexedDB or a cache and you rotated the key elsewhere, the SW may still be using the old value until it is updated. Two mitigations: 1) make the SW check a short-lived cache key/ETag so it refreshes quickly, or 2) avoid persisting long-lived plaintext keys in the SW.

maria_support: For immediate unblock: 1) Ask affected users to unregister the service worker / hard refresh (Chrome: DevTools -> Application -> Service Workers -> Unregister) OR clear site data. 2) Redeploy corrected SW that reads the key from a live source (e.g., ephemeral token endpoint) or explicitly deletes old cached auth.

jb_customer: We unregistered the SW and users are back to normal. We'll push a fix to stop caching Authorization and rotate keys again to be safe. Thanks! :party_parrot: 

maria_support: Glad that worked. For our records, I'll flag this as a guidance doc candidate: "Avoid caching API keys in service workers; prefer short-lived session tokens." If you want, we can share a short snippet for your repo.

jb_customer: That would be great. Also one more note — we saw 401 payload body: {"error":"invalid_api_key","message":"API key not found or expired"}. Might help others triage.

maria_support: Perfect — thanks for the details. I'll close the ticket once you confirm the fix is in prod.

deploy-bot: deploy web-service@c8f7a2 successful — rolled back SW to v3.3.7 (to remove caching logic)

jb_customer: Confirmed fix in prod. All good now. Thanks everyone :thumbsup:
